<p>Australian court records released Wednesday show Naveed Akram, 24, faces 19 additional charges over the Dec. 14, 2025, attack on a Hanukkah celebration at Sydney’s Bondi Beach, where 15 people were killed and dozens more were injured. Akram had already been charged with 59 counts, including murder, attempted murder and committing a terrorist act. The new charges include multiple counts of shooting with intent to murder, wounding with intent to murder and discharging a firearm with intent to resist arrest. Some sources listed 10 counts of shooting with intent to murder and six counts of discharging a firearm with intent to resist arrest. Akram has not yet indicated how he will plead. His father, Sajid Akram, 50, whom police described as an alleged co-conspirator, was shot dead by police during the attack.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/myYMymhsQcrdZmSDH2FgJUn_q1rlqDvHTg8nZMKNVSssMA93qbyFmeqzpK52xIlJB6C45zEscohbxQOqbGuNk1fcSe0TSj3vrc2Xw6DTaeyu' alt='Mourners pay a floral tribute to Bondi Beach shooting victims at the Bondi Pavillion in Sydney on December 15, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Remote appearance: Akram was due to appear by video link from jail at Sydney’s Downing Centre Local Court on Wednesday.</li><li>Improvised explosive devices: Police allege the attackers threw improvised explosive devices into a group of people, but the devices did not detonate.</li><li>Public inquiry: A broad inquiry into Australia’s deadliest mass shooting in almost three decades opened public hearings this week.</li></ul><blockquote>The sharp spike of anti-Semitism that we have witnessed in Australia has been mirrored in other Western countries and seems clearly linked to events in the Middle East - Virginia Bell</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Australian police: Infobae reported police said the massacre was inspired by the Islamic State group. (<a href='https://www.infobae.com/america/mundo/2026/05/06/el-autor-del-tiroteo-en-bondi-beach-enfrentara-